course: dictionary
lesson: 12
title: Able: what you can do
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Able is a small word with a big job. If you are able to do something, you have the skill, strength, time or chance to do it. «I will be able to swim» means swimming is something you can do in the future, once you have learned how.
explain: Look at the words around able to find its meaning. «You should be able to see the steps and your feet when you carry something on the stairs.» The clue is see the steps and your feet: being able to see them means you can carry the thing safely. Be able to is used where can does not work, such as after will or have: «I have been able to read since I was five.»
explain: Able can also describe a person who is clever or good at things, like an able pupil. Many words end in -able and mean can be done: washable means it can be washed, and breakable means it can be broken, so handle it with care.
fact: Able means having the skill, strength, time or chance to do something.
fact: «Be able to» is used where «can» does not work, as in «I will be able to swim».
fact: «Able» can also describe someone who is clever or good at things, like an able pupil.
fact: Many words ending in -able mean can be done: washable, readable, breakable.
fact: Useful phrases include «not able to», «able-bodied» and «more able».
exercise: «You should be able to see the steps and your feet when you carry something on the stairs.» What does able mean here? | Too tired to climb the stairs | Forgetting the steps | Clever at climbing | *Can see them clearly
exercise: «I will be able to swim next summer.» Which words show that swimming is possible in the future? | I will | next summer | swim | *be able to
exercise: Washable means what? | Not washing at all | Washed up in the sink | *Can be washed | Very dirty and muddy
test: Tom says, «I have been able to read since I was five.» What does able suggest? | He cannot read at all | He read one book only | *He has had the skill to read for years | He reads very slowly
test: Mia says «be able to» always means the same as «can», so we can swap them every time. Is she right? | Yes, always | No, because able is a colour | *No, can does not work after will or have | Yes, but only in stories
exam: Which sentence uses able correctly to mean has the chance or ability to do something? | Able the shelf reach you. | I am ablest at shelves. | *Are you able to reach the shelf? | The able shelf is very big.
